{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1c606213de7941b","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/pkg/github_test.go has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01b06f2b-f2ff-455b-be03-9ccba45d0025","uuid":"c5102ea9-f4de-47a2-89f0-d9deb5fe7028","timestamp":"2026-11-27T14:21:59.280Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/pkg/github_test.go","oldString":"\tIt(\"does not retry a plain 403\", func() {","newString":"\tIt(\"bounds the accumulated retry sleep inside the client timeout\", func() {\n\t\t// MaxWait bounds a single wait, so a persistent short Retry-After is\n\t\t// slept MaxAttempts times inside one client.Do. The one-second bound\n\t\t// keeps that budget at five seconds, inside the 15-second client\n\t\t// timeout: the exhausted retries surface the real 429, not a\n\t\t// context-deadline cancellation.\n\t\tvar requests int32\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tatomic.AddInt32(&requests, 1)\n\t\t\tw.Header().Set(\"Retry-After\", \"1\")\n\t\t\tw.WriteHeader(http.StatusTooManyRequests)\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\tstart := time.Now()\n\t\t_, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\telapsed := time.Since(start)\n\t\tExpect(err).NotTo(BeNil())\n\t\tExpect(err.Error()).To(ContainSubstring(\"HTTP 429\"))\n\t\tExpect(err.Error()).NotTo(ContainSubstring(\"context deadline exceeded\"))\n\t\tExpect(atomic.LoadInt32(&requests)).To(Equal(int32(6)))\n\t\tExpect(elapsed).To(BeNumerically(\"<\", 15*time.Second))\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"does not retry a plain 403\", func() {","originalFile":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Benjamin Borbe All rights reserved.\n// Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style\n// license that can be found in the LICENSE file.\n\npackage pkg_test\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\t\"net/http/httptest\"\n\t\"sync/atomic\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t. \"github.com/onsi/ginkgo/v2\"\n\t. \"github.com/onsi/gomega\"\n\n\t\"github.com/bborbe/github-dark-factory-agent/pkg\"\n)\n\nvar _ = Describe(\"GitHubClient\", func() {\n\tvar ctx context.Context\n\n\tBeforeEach(func() {\n\t\tctx = context.Background()\n\t\t// currentToken prefers the live process value, so an ambient GH_TOKEN\n\t\t// (a dev shell, or the dark-factory execution phase that exports it)\n\t\t// would override the captured token these specs assert on. Each spec\n\t\t// opts in to the value it wants to observe.\n\t\tGinkgoT().Setenv(\"GH_TOKEN\", \"\")\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"prefers the live GH_TOKEN over the token captured at construction\", func() {\n\t\t// Installation tokens expire after 60 minutes and the execution phase\n\t\t// re-mints before its push; ai_review runs later in the same process, so\n\t\t// its REST calls must use the refreshed value, not the startup one.\n\t\tGinkgoT().Setenv(\"GH_TOKEN\", \"live\")\n\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tExpect(r.Header.Get(\"Authorization\")).To(Equal(\"token live\"))\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(`{\"draft\":true,\"head\":{\"sha\":\"abc123\"}}`))\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"captured\", srv.URL)\n\t\t_, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).To(BeNil())\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"sends no Authorization header when neither a live nor a captured token exists\", func() {\n\t\t// Public repos need no credential; an empty header must not become a\n\t\t// bare \"token \" prefix that GitHub rejects.\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tExpect(r.Header.Get(\"Authorization\")).To(BeEmpty())\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(`{\"draft\":true,\"head\":{\"sha\":\"abc123\"}}`))\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"\", srv.URL)\n\t\t_, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).To(BeNil())\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"GetPullRequest parses draft flag and head sha\", func() {\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tExpect(r.URL.Path).To(Equal(\"/repos/bborbe/sandbox/pulls/7\"))\n\t\t\tExpect(r.Header.Get(\"Authorization\")).To(Equal(\"token tok\"))\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(`{\"draft\":true,\"head\":{\"sha\":\"abc123\"}}`))\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\tinfo, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).To(BeNil())\n\t\tExpect(info.Draft).To(BeTrue())\n\t\tExpect(info.HeadSHA).To(Equal(\"abc123\"))\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"ListPullRequestFiles parses filenames\", func() {\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write(\n\t\t\t\t[]byte(`[{\"filename\":\"specs/in-progress/foo.md\"},{\"filename\":\"README.md\"}]`),\n\t\t\t)\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\tfiles, err := client.ListPullRequestFiles(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).To(BeNil())\n\t\tExpect(files).To(ConsistOf(\"specs/in-progress/foo.md\", \"README.md\"))\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"returns an error on a non-200 response\", func() {\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tw.WriteHeader(http.StatusNotFound)\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(`{\"message\":\"Not Found\"}`))\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\t_, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).NotTo(BeNil())\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"retries a short 429 and succeeds\", func() {\n\t\tvar requests int32\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tif atomic.AddInt32(&requests, 1) == 1 {\n\t\t\t\tw.Header().Set(\"Retry-After\", \"1\")\n\t\t\t\tw.WriteHeader(http.StatusTooManyRequests)\n\t\t\t\treturn\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(`{\"draft\":true,\"head\":{\"sha\":\"abc123\"}}`))\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\tinfo, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).To(BeNil())\n\t\tExpect(info.HeadSHA).To(Equal(\"abc123\"))\n\t\tExpect(atomic.LoadInt32(&requests)).To(Equal(int32(2)))\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"defers a 429 whose requested wait exceeds MaxWait\", func() {\n\t\tvar requests int32\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tatomic.AddInt32(&requests, 1)\n\t\t\tw.Header().Set(\"Retry-After\", \"60\")\n\t\t\tw.WriteHeader(http.StatusTooManyRequests)\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\tstart := time.Now()\n\t\t_, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\telapsed := time.Since(start)\n\t\tExpect(err).NotTo(BeNil())\n\t\tExpect(err.Error()).To(ContainSubstring(\"HTTP 429\"))\n\t\tExpect(atomic.LoadInt32(&requests)).To(Equal(int32(1)))\n\t\tExpect(elapsed).To(BeNumerically(\"<\", 5*time.Second))\n\t})\n\n\tIt(\"does not retry a plain 403\", func() {\n\t\tvar requests int32\n\t\tsrv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {\n\t\t\tatomic.AddInt32(&requests, 1)\n\t\t\tw.WriteHeader(http.StatusForbidden)\n\t\t\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(`{\"message\":\"Forbidden\"}`))\n\t\t}))\n\t\tDeferCleanup(srv.Close)\n\n\t\tclient := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)\n\t\t_, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)\n\t\tExpect(err).NotTo(BeNil())\n\t\tExpect(err.Error()).To(ContainSubstring(\"HTTP 403\"))\n\t\tExpect(atomic.LoadInt32(&requests)).To(Equal(int32(1)))\n\t})\n})\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":139,"oldLines":6,"newStart":139,"newLines":31,"lines":["     Expect(elapsed).To(BeNumerically(\"<\", 5*time.Second))","   })"," ","+  It(\"bounds the accumulated retry sleep inside the client timeout\", func() {","+    // MaxWait bounds a single wait, so a persistent short Retry-After is","+    // slept MaxAttempts times inside one client.Do. The one-second bound","+    // keeps that budget at five seconds, inside the 15-second client","+    // timeout: the exhausted retries surface the real 429, not a","+    // context-deadline cancellation.","+    var requests int32","+    srv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {","+      atomic.AddInt32(&requests, 1)","+      w.Header().Set(\"Retry-After\", \"1\")","+      w.WriteHeader(http.StatusTooManyRequests)","+    }))","+    DeferCleanup(srv.Close)","+","+    client := pkg.NewGitHubClientWithBaseURL(\"tok\", srv.URL)","+    start := time.Now()","+    _, err := client.GetPullRequest(ctx, \"bborbe\", \"sandbox\", 7)","+    elapsed := time.Since(start)","+    Expect(err).NotTo(BeNil())","+    Expect(err.Error()).To(ContainSubstring(\"HTTP 429\"))","+    Expect(err.Error()).NotTo(ContainSubstring(\"context deadline exceeded\"))","+    Expect(atomic.LoadInt32(&requests)).To(Equal(int32(6)))","+    Expect(elapsed).To(BeNumerically(\"<\", 15*time.Second))","+  })","+","   It(\"does not retry a plain 403\", func() {","     var requests int32","     srv := httptest.NewServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4010f2a99bb3e4d","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/CHANGELOG.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01b06f2b-f2ff-455b-be03-9ccba45d0025","uuid":"14089ebd-7686-445d-bbca-f26e2927ca30","timestamp":"2026-11-27T14:21:59.293Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/CHANGELOG.md","oldString":"waits over the five-second `MaxWait` bound are deferred so the caller sees the real rate-limit response rather than a context-deadline error","newString":"the per-wait `MaxWait` bound is one second so the total sleep budget across the default five attempts (5 × 1s) stays inside both client timeouts, and a longer server-requested wait is deferred so the caller sees the real rate-limit response rather than a context-deadline error","originalFile":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.0.0/),\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n\n## Unreleased\n\n- feat: route the GitHub REST client and the GH-token preflight through `bborbe/http`'s rate-limit retry round tripper (`github.com/bborbe/http` v1.28.1), so a 403/429 from `api.github.com` is retried after the server-requested wait instead of ending the work item; waits over the five-second `MaxWait` bound are deferred so the caller sees the real rate-limit response rather than a context-deadline error\n\n## v0.8.7\n\n- chore: remove orphaned section comments left in `.trivyignore` by the stale-ignore sweep\n\n## v0.8.6\n\n- chore: remove 10 stale vulnerability ignores (withdrawn advisory, module absent from go.mod, or version outside the affected range)\n\n## v0.8.5\n\n- fix: bump the baked dark-factory pin from `v0.199.6` to `v0.199.7`, which stashes the dirty spec bookkeeping around the branch workflow's `git merge origin/<default>` sync. `syncWithRemoteViaDeps` fetched and merged before `setupInPlaceBranch` persisted the generator's spec-status write, so `git merge` aborted with `Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge: specs/in-progress/…`; the prompt failed permanently, the lifecycle drain aborted, and the agent exited 1 — surfaced as a bare `pod_crash_no_stdout`. `syncWithRemoteViaDeps` now stashes `SpecBookkeepingPrefixes` before the merge and pops after. Both the CLI build and the plugin marketplace clone move together, since they are pinned to the same `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION`.\n\n## v0.8.4\n\n- fix: bump the baked dark-factory pin from `v0.199.5` to `v0.199.6`, which approves a generated prompt set in the order the prompt-creator prefixed it. `diffFiles` ranged over the inbox snapshot map, and the auto-approve loop approves one prompt at a time — `ApproveFromInbox` strips the creator's prefix and `NormalizeFilenames` assigns the next free number — so execution numbers were assigned in Go's randomised map order. A spec declaring `service → route → hardening` came out `186-route` / `187-hardening` / `188-service`, running the hardening prompt before the service it exercises. `diffFiles` now sorts by the numeric prefix on each filename (parsed, so `10-` follows `2-`), unprefixed last, full path as the tie-break. Both the CLI build and the plugin marketplace clone move together, since they are pinned to the same `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION`.\n\n## v0.8.3\n\n- fix: bump the baked dark-factory pin from `v0.199.4` to `v0.199.5`, which carries the cross-branch stash fix. `brancher.StashInPaths` treated an empty `git status --porcelain` result as a tracked change — `strings.Split(\"\", \"\\n\")` yields `[\"\"]`, neither `??` nor `!!` — so every `SpecBookkeepingPrefixes` entry joined the `git stash push` pathspec and any bookkeeping dir the repo never created aborted the whole prompt setup with `pathspec ':(prefix:0)specs/rejected' did not match any file(s) known to git`. That killed the `bborbe/vault-ui` PR #232 cluster run on 2026-11-23T10:59Z. `StashInPaths` now returns early on an empty status, mirroring the guard `CommitUncommittedInPaths` already carried. Both the CLI build and the plugin marketplace clone move together, since they are pinned to the same `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION`.\n\n## v0.8.2\n\n- fix: bump the baked dark-factory pin from `v0.199.3` to `v0.199.4`, which carries the cross-branch spec-status write fix. A cluster run that starts on the default branch and must switch onto a PR branch now lands the spec-status write in the target branch's history (stash → switch → pop → commit) instead of refusing at branch setup with `working tree is not clean`. Both the CLI build and the plugin marketplace clone move together, since they are pinned to the same `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION`.\n\n## v0.8.1\n\n- fix: bump the baked dark-factory pin from `v0.198.11` to `v0.199.3`, which carries the spec-status write-before-branch-switch fix. The image bakes dark-factory at build time, so a release alone never reached the running agent — every cluster run kept executing the old refusal (`working tree is not clean; cannot switch to branch`) at branch setup. Both the CLI build and the plugin marketplace clone move together, since they are pinned to the same `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION`.\n\n## v0.8.0\n\n- fix: **`REPOS_PATH` and `WORK_PATH` are now required at startup instead of defaulting to the unwritable `/repos` and `/work`.** The pod runs as the image's unprivileged `node` user against a root-owned `/`, so an omitted variable resolved to an unwritable root and the run died minutes later at `git worktree add` with `fatal: could not create leading directories of '/work/<job>/.git': Permission denied` — 12 implement tasks hit it on prod between 2026-10-19 and 2026-11-02, and nothing in the failure named the variable. Both fields lose their `default:` tag and gain `required:\"true\"`, so the argument parser fails immediately with `Required field empty, define parameter work-path or define env WORK_PATH`. Both nuke stages already pin both variables, so no running deployment changes. `cmd/run-task` keeps its `~/.cache/github-dark-factory-agent/{repos,work}` fallback — that binary is the local CLI entry point, where a default is the point.\n\n## v0.7.2\n\n- fix: **a planning precondition that cannot be cleared by a re-run now parks as `needs_input` instead of `failed`.** `failed` is defined as transient — the controller retries it, incrementing `trigger_count` until `max_triggers` holds the record — so a record whose PR head had advanced since emit re-ran the identical refusal until the budget was gone. Measured on sun 2026-11-17: record `291e00a7` (PR `bborbe/agent-task-controller#163`, ref `67c699b6`) failed 07:44–07:49Z as `pod_crash_no_stdout (Error/1)`, because the process exits non-zero for `failed` but returns nil for every other status. The four permanent preconditions — ref≠HEAD, PR not a draft, `.dark-factory.yaml` missing, no approved-not-completed spec in the diff — now return `needs_input`, which the deliverer writes as `status: in_progress` with the assignee cleared and the phase preserved, so the record parks for a human on its first trigger. Clone and GitHub API errors keep `failed`; those are genuinely transient.\n\n## v0.7.1\n\n- fix: count an inbox prompt as work in flight only when it can still advance one of the run's requested specs, so an unrelated `status: draft` prompt can no longer hold the lifecycle drain to the Job deadline. `hasInboxPrompts` was a bare `prompts/*.md` glob, so on 2026-11-14 four drafts for other specs (023, 010, 021, bab89662) sat in the inbox of `bborbe/agent-task-executor` and both `drained()` and `findStalledSpec()` read them as work in flight for a run that requested only `bug-nuke-dev-fm`: the drain never completed, the stall detector never fired, and a Job whose prompt had already reported success was killed at its deadline and republished as `failed`. `rejected`/`cancelled`/`completed` never count, and an unreadable or not-yet-written prompt still counts so a mid-write prompt is never read as parked. The auto-approve window is preserved because the prompt the run just generated is linked to a requested spec; the daemon's auto-approve setting is not consulted, since every lifecycle run passes `--auto-approve-prompts`.\n\n## v0.7.0\n\n- feat: add a `check-task-link` gate that fails a PR whose body carries no `Task: <task_identifier>` line, and install a pinned, checksum-verified Trivy release so `test` stops failing on the retired keyring helper\n\n## v0.6.3\n\n- fix: bump the `go` directive to 1.27.4 and the Dockerfile build image to `golang:1.27.4`, so the image and `go.mod` name the same toolchain. The update-go agent could not proceed because `make precommit` failed with exit 2 and no parseable findings, which it treats as a broken gate and never retries — the real cause was the stale toolchain, not the code. `GOSEC_VERSION` moves to v2.30.0 in step with the canonical `tools.env`; this repo runs gosec inside golangci-lint rather than standalone, so the pin is kept for sync only.\n\n## v0.6.2\n\n- fix: link the pinned dark-factory clone to `~/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/dark-factory` so the authoring-rule paths the agents hardcode resolve — a local-path `marketplace add` never creates that directory, so prompt generation ran with no guides\n\n## v0.6.1\n\n- chore: restate the `do-not-merge` gate message to name its bypass actors — the Admin repository role and Integration `3907226`, both `bypass_mode: always` in the `master-protection` ruleset — instead of asserting an unqualified block\n\n## v0.6.0\n\n- feat: **build the release image on a cluster (`BUILD_TARGET=sun`), not only locally.** `make build` had a single arm — a local `docker build` — so publishing a release required a Docker daemon and `docker push` on the workstation. An agent is refused `docker push`, which made a release image unpublishable without a human at the keyboard. `Makefile.docker` now carries two arms, ported from `bborbe/a2a-worker-controller` and `bborbe/agent-claude`: `BUILD_TARGET=local` (the default, unchanged behaviour) and any other value, which renders `hack/build-job.yaml` as a Job on that cluster and pushes from inside it. The vendored `hack/` set is `a2a-worker-controller`'s hardened copy — templated namespace, `case`-based value whitelisting, no service-account token — rather than `agent-claude`'s older one. The cluster arm refuses to run without an explicit `VERSION` (the Makefile default is the newest release tag, so a defaulted build would overwrite a published image with one built from a different commit) and refuses to push a `vX.Y.Z` tag from any `REF` other than that tag. `upload` and `clean` are guarded so a remote build does not fail at a push step for an image that was never local. `Dockerfile` gains a `go mod vendor` step in its build stage: the local arm got `vendor/` from `check-go-mod`, but the cluster arm clones a git ref where `vendor/` is gitignored, so `-mod=vendor` would have failed there. `make precommit` now runs `shellcheck -S warning` over `hack/*.sh`, since those scripts handle the teamvault→Secret credential path\n\n## v0.5.14\n\n- fix: **redact the `AnthropicAuthToken` value from the startup argument dump.** The root binary's `application` struct carried `display:\"password\"` — a tag the argument printer does not implement. It honours only `display:\"hidden\"` and `display:\"length\"`, so every other value fell through to the plaintext branch (`Argument: %s '%v'`) and each Job start wrote the live model-router credential in cleartext into the cluster Job log. The field now carries `display:\"length\"`, matching the sibling in `cmd/run-task`, so the dump names the key and its length and never the value. A regression spec drives the real printer over the real struct and fails if the redaction is removed\n\n## v0.5.13\n\n- test: **cover the per-prompt setup fetch, not only the push.** `v0.5.12` moved the credential to a file-backed git helper, but only the push site had a regression spec — the fetch site (the one that failed first on 2026-11-03) was pinned by nothing. The new spec starts with token `A`, lets the lifecycle refresher mint `B`, and asserts the credential `git credential fill` resolves *during* the run is `B` — the A→B shape a \"file is non-empty\" check would not discriminate. No production code changes\n\n## v0.5.12\n\n- fix: **install a file-backed git credential helper so a long lifecycle run never uses an expired token.** The GitHub App installation token lives 60 minutes and is minted once at startup; `gh auth setup-git` binds it into a helper that resolves `GH_TOKEN` from the environment of whatever runs git, and the dark-factory daemon captured that environment when it was started — so no refresh in the parent process can reach it. A token file has no such lifetime: git re-reads it at every credential lookup. Observed 2026-11-03 on `bborbe/trading#433`: token minted 18:10Z, the daemon's own `git fetch origin` refused at 19:15Z with `Invalid username or token. Password authentication is not supported`\n\n## v0.5.11\n\n- fix: **exit non-zero when the agent publishes `Status: failed`.** The result was still published to the task, but the process returned nil, so the Job completed and the executor — which records a phase success on exit 0 alone (`IsJobSucceeded` in `agent-task-executor`'s `job_watcher`) and never reads the published Status — wrote a *phase success* for a run that had failed. Once the success grace expired the task was parked at `human_review` as though a human had parked it deliberately. Observed 2026-11-03 on task `ceb79961`: a Job that published `Status: failed` for `git clone --bare: Failed to connect to github.com port 443` exited 0 and parked the task. `needs_input` is deliberately unchanged — parking for a human answer is that status's intent\n\n## v0.5.10\n\n- chore: update github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.138.0, github.com/bborbe/cqrs to v0.6.15, github.com/bborbe/errors to v1.6.6, github.com/bborbe/kafka to v1.29.11, github.com/bborbe/maintainer to v0.51.0, github.com/bborbe/sentry to v1.10.7, github.com/bborbe/service to v1.11.2, github.com/bborbe/time to v1.27.25, github.com/bborbe/vault-cli to v0.186.2, github.com/onsi/ginkgo/v2 to v2.34.0\n\n## v0.5.9\n\n- fix: re-mint the GitHub credential immediately before the push. The installation token is minted once at startup (`resolveAuth` → `githubapp.MintIAT`) and GitHub App installation tokens expire after 60 minutes, while the execution phase routinely runs longer — so the final `git push` was refused with `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is not supported for Git operation`, after the implementation itself had succeeded. Measured 2026-11-02 on `bborbe/attention-controller#166`: token minted 14:54Z, push attempted 16:07Z, 73 minutes later. The execution step now calls a `refreshToken` seam right before `PushBranch` and exports the fresh value into the process env the push inherits; a refresh failure is logged and the step continues, so a short run that still holds a valid token is unaffected. The credential consumers that captured the startup token — `pkg/git`'s `cmdEnv()` and the GitHub REST client — now read the live `GH_TOKEN` first, so the refresh also reaches `headAfterPush` and ai_review's own `GetPullRequest` / worktree fetch, not only the push. **Not covered:** ai_review's Claude subprocess still receives the startup token, because its env map is built once from `resolvedToken` and the Claude runner applies an allowlist rather than inheriting `os.Environ()`. That path only matters if the review model shells `gh pr view/diff` after the 60-minute mark; the review step's own REST and git calls use the refreshed token. (`Permission to … denied` would have meant the App lacked write access — it did not, which is what ruled that out.)\n\n## v0.5.8\n\n- fix: pass the egress proxy environment through to git subprocesses. `cmdEnv()` returned only `HOME`, `PATH` and `GH_TOKEN`, so a clone from a sandbox that admits egress only through the allowlisting proxy dialled `github.com:443` directly and was refused by the NetworkPolicy in 2–9 ms — every dark-factory run on nuke prod failed at `planning: ensure worktree` before any LLM work started. The six proxy variables (`HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY`, `NO_PROXY` and their lowercase forms) are appended when set, mirroring `github-pr-review-agent`, `github-releaser-agent` and `github-update-go-agent`, which took the same fix on 2026-10-28; an unset variable still contributes no entry, so the allowlist does not become a pass-through.\n\n## v0.5.7\n\n- fix: update github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.132.5 (with cqrs v0.6.13, errors v1.6.4, kafka v1.28.6, sentry v1.10.6, time v1.27.18, vault-cli v0.178.3 pulled in transitively) so the result deliverer stops restoring a stale phase. A result that advances no phase no longer republishes the Job-start phase over a park: the Kafka payload omits `phase` on those branches, so the controller's on-disk-first merge keeps the on-disk value, and the file deliverer carries the on-disk phase forward. Closes the prod-vs-sun duplicate class measured on v7.\n\n## v0.5.6\n\n- fix: fail a lifecycle run that is stranded in **any** non-terminal spec status, not only `prompted`. `drained()` accepts only `verifying`/`completed`, but the stall detector compared against `prompted` alone, so a spec left at `draft` or `approved` kept `drained()` false forever while nothing could advance it — the daemon logged `nothing to do, waiting for changes` and the run held its only agent slot until `activeDeadlineSeconds: 5400`. Measured 2026-10-22 17:28Z→18:34Z: spec `063-a2a-default-cluster` sat at `verifying` while a sibling spec never advanced, **45 idle iterations over ~66 min**, on v0.5.5 — a build that already contained every earlier guard, which is why the previous grace-window fix did not cover it. The detector now skips only `verifying`/`completed`/**`generating`** and reports any other status with no prompt in flight. `generating` stays excluded deliberately: dark-factory drives it and `runner/lifecycle.go` resumes or resets it after a restart, so aborting there would kill healthy work — and while **any** spec under `specs/in-progress` is `generating`, nothing is reported at all, because dark-factory generates prompts one spec at a time (`specWatcher` mutex + sequential startup scan), so a sibling legitimately waits at `approved` for the whole generation (~7 min). Flagging that sibling would abort a healthy multi-spec run 30 s in. `stallGracePolls` 3 → 10 (30 s at the 3 s poll) because `draft`/`approved` have no prompt to watch, so the grace must outlast the daemon's own pickup tick as well as the `prompted` flicker — still turning a 90-minute slot-hold into 30 seconds. The error now names the spec's real status instead of hard-coding `prompted`.\n\n## v0.5.5\n\n- fix: skip the ai_review worktree post-conditions when execution adopted an already-pushed branch. The adopt path routes to ai_review without creating a worktree, but ai_review checked out the task's stale `ref` and demanded every matched spec be completed there — completions the original run had already pushed to the branch, so the check could never pass and every adoption ended in `spec … is not completed (execution did not finish)`. Found on the nuke-dev replay: the run logged `\"adopted\": true` and then failed one step later. An adopted result now goes straight to the read-only PR verdict; the draft-PR and diff-vs-spec checks still run. Its `## Review` records only those two in `checks_passed` (with `adopted: true`) — not the `specs_completed` / `no_prompts_in_flight` gates the adopted path never evaluates, since that artifact is the human's draft→ready sign-off and must not attest to checks that did not run.\n\n## v0.5.4\n\n- fix: adopt an already-pushed branch head instead of re-running the whole lifecycle. A crash after `PushBranch` but before `## Result` is delivered left the remote branch ahead of the task's `ref`; the re-run repeated the full LLM job (~30 min of prompts, reproduced on nuke dev 2026-10-21) and then failed non-fast-forward with `! [rejected] HEAD -> <branch> (non-fast-forward)`, leaving a spurious failed task. The execution step now compares the remote branch head with `ref` before `EnsureWorktree`: a head that moved **and** descends from `ref` is adopted (no lifecycle, `## Result` written citing the head with `adopted: true` and `prompts_executed: 0`); a head that moved but does not descend from `ref` returns needs-input naming both shas rather than claiming another push's work.\n\n## v0.5.3\n\n- chore: bump the baked `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION` v0.198.9 → v0.198.11 (CLI + plugin, one pinned clone). v0.198.10 drops `model: opus` from the six `agents/*.md`, so a subagent inherits the session model instead of resolving to an Anthropic name the router maps to an adaptive-thinking-only backend, which answered every dispatch with `API Error: 400`. v0.198.11 adds `Task` to `allowed-tools` in `commands/create-prompt.md` and `commands/create-spec.md`, so the prompt-creator / spec-creator **agents** can be invoked through the Task tool; without it the command fell back to a Skill-tool call and failed with `Unknown skill: dark-factory:prompt-creator`. Both fixes reach this image through the plugin clone, which is pinned to the same tag.\n\n## v0.5.2\n\n- chore: update Go to 1.27.3 and github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.112.0, github.com/bborbe/kafka to v1.28.4, github.com/bborbe/sentry to v1.10.5, github.com/bborbe/vault-cli to v0.177.0\n\n## v0.5.1\n\n- fix: **`waitForDrain` fails fast when a requested spec is stranded at `prompted` with no prompt referencing it**, instead of holding the Job's only agent slot until the lifecycle deadline. ⚠️ dark-factory advances a spec out of `prompted` only through its spec-link scan (`CheckAndComplete`), which no-ops when no prompt carries `spec:` — so a prompt authored without the link leaves the spec unreachable and every later daemon tick a no-op. `drained()` cannot see this: it counts in-flight prompts, and a stranded run has none. Observed 2026-10-20 on nuke dev: a fallback hand-authored prompt with `spec: \"\"` executed to `exit_code=0`, the spec stayed `prompted`, and the run burned the whole deadline before reporting `lifecycle did not drain before deadline` over work that had actually succeeded. The new `findStalledSpec` requires three consecutive polls (9s at the 3s interval) so a healthy run — whose spec is momentarily `prompted` while its prompt is auto-approved — is never mistaken for a stalled one.\n\n## v0.5.0\n\n- chore: bump the baked `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION` v0.192.4 → v0.198.9 (CLI + plugin, one pinned clone). v0.198.9 makes the `spec:` link mandatory for spec-derived prompts and enforces it in the generator's `finalizePrompted`: an empty link is backfilled with an ERROR naming the file, and a batch in which no prompt links the spec now fails so the spec resets to `approved` instead of being marked `prompted`. Without it the cluster route stalls silently — the generated prompt carried no link, `CheckAndComplete` found none and no-oped, the spec sat at `prompted` forever, and the lifecycle drain burned to its deadline (nuke-dev, `bborbe/go-skeleton` PR #128, 2026-10-20). ⚠️ The jump spans six minor versions, so the CLI surface this agent drives was re-checked at the new tag: `daemon` with `--set backend=local`, `--set hideGit=true`, `--set autoGeneratePrompts=true`, `--auto-approve-prompts`, `--skip-preflight`, `--skip-healthcheck`, `--set model=…`, plus `spec complete <id>` — all present.\n\n## v0.4.8\n\n- chore: update github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.103.1, github.com/bborbe/cqrs to v0.6.12, github.com/bborbe/errors to v1.6.3, github.com/bborbe/kafka to v1.28.3, github.com/bborbe/maintainer to v0.50.12, github.com/bborbe/sentry to v1.10.4, github.com/bborbe/service to v1.11.0, github.com/bborbe/time to v1.27.17, github.com/bborbe/vault-cli to v0.174.0, github.com/onsi/ginkgo/v2 to v2.33.1, github.com/onsi/gomega to v1.44.0, github.com/prometheus/client_golang to v1.25.0\n\n## v0.4.7\n\n- fix: **Go 1.27.2 now passes `make precommit`.** The standalone `errcheck:` and `gosec:` targets are both retired and dropped from `check:`, and both linters now run embedded in golangci-lint, which `.golangci.yml` already enabled. `GOLANGCI_LINT_VERSION` moves v2.13.1 → v2.14.0, the `go` directive and `Dockerfile` move to 1.27.2, and `golang.org/x/net` moves v0.58.0 → v0.60.0. ⚠️ **Neither target was repairable, which is why both are removed rather than repointed.** `go run github.com/kisielk/errcheck@v1.20.0` and `go run github.com/securego/gosec/v2/cmd/gosec@$(GOSEC_VERSION)` each build in their **own** module context, pinning a `golang.org/x/tools` reader that stops at export-data version 4 while the 1.27.2 toolchain emits version 5, so each dies with `internal error: package \"X\" without types was imported` at any `go.mod` directive. Dropping the `@$(ERRCHECK_VERSION)` pin does not rescue it either: `precommit` runs `ensure` → `go mod tidy` first, and tidy strips a require nothing imports. ⚠️ **Coverage is preserved, not traded away.** `.golangci.yml` gains `settings.errcheck.exclude-functions` carrying the retired target's `-ignore '(Close|Write|Fprint)'` list, and `settings.gosec.excludes: [G104]` carrying `-exclude=G104`. The `go` directive bump is load-bearing rather than tidy: it is what clears the nine `stdlib@v1.27.1 -> v1.27.2` advisories that `osv-scanner` reports, and it could not move while the standalone targets ran. Also bumps `osv-scanner` v2.3.1 → v2.6.0: the pinned v2.3.1 panics during call-graph analysis in `golang.org/x/tools@v0.38.0/go/ssa`, and v2.6.0 carries a fixed x/tools.\n\n## v0.4.6\n\n- fix: `make build` refuses to stamp a version onto a tree that is not that version's tag (`check-version-tag`, escape hatch `ALLOW_UNTAGGED_BUILD=1`). `VERSION` defaults to the newest tag repo-wide, so an operator-run build from an untagged or older tree silently republishes under the newest tag. The guard compares `git describe --exact-match HEAD` against `$(VERSION)` and exits non-zero on mismatch.\n\n## v0.4.5\n\n- chore: update github.com/bborbe/service to v1.10.13, github.com/bborbe/vault-cli to v0.122.3\n\n## v0.4.4\n\n- chore: update Go to 1.27.1 and github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.87.1, github.com/bborbe/cqrs to v0.6.10, github.com/bborbe/errors to v1.6.0, github.com/bborbe/kafka to v1.25.13, github.com/bborbe/maintainer to v0.50.6, github.com/bborbe/sentry to v1.10.1, github.com/bborbe/service to v1.10.12, github.com/bborbe/time to v1.27.13, github.com/bborbe/vault-cli to v0.122.2, github.com/onsi/gomega to v1.43.0\n\n## v0.4.3\n\n- fix: update `golang.org/x/crypto` to v0.56.0 — clears the vulnerability gate blocking this repo's CI\n- fix: `Dockerfile` `ARG DOCKER_REGISTRY` default now points at `docker.prod.nuke.benjamin-borbe.de:443` instead of the decommissioned `docker.quant.benjamin-borbe.de:443`. Inert in CI (which passes `DOCKER_REGISTRY` explicitly) but a bare local `docker build` silently targeted a dead host\n\n## v0.4.2\n\n- chore: update github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.83.1, github.com/bborbe/maintainer to v0.50.3\n\n## v0.4.1\n\n- chore: update Go to 1.27.0 and github.com/bborbe/agent to v0.83.0, github.com/bborbe/cqrs to v0.6.8, github.com/bborbe/errors to v1.5.21, github.com/bborbe/kafka to v1.25.9, github.com/bborbe/maintainer to v0.50.2, github.com/bborbe/sentry to v1.9.27, github.com/bborbe/service to v1.10.9, github.com/bborbe/time to v1.27.10, github.com/bborbe/vault-cli to v0.116.2\n\n## v0.4.0\n\n- fix: raise the lifecycle timeout default 30m→90m and make it configurable, so real multi-prompt feature runs (generation ~7m + per-prompt execute 10-15m + fix loops) no longer hit the deadline mid-execution. New `LIFECYCLE_TIMEOUT` env override (Go duration) sets the deployment default; a per-task `lifecycle_timeout_minutes:` frontmatter field overrides for that task only. Both are resolved via `ResolveLifecycleTimeoutFromEnv` / `ResolveLifecycleTimeout` and threaded through `NewExecutionRunnerWithTimeout`. Invalid/absent values fall back safely (never zero).\n- fix: handle ignored `os.Setenv`/`os.Unsetenv` error returns in the lifecycle-timeout tests so the standalone errcheck target passes.\n- fix: unexport `resolveLifecycleTimeoutFromEnv`/`resolveLifecycleTimeout` (factory-pattern rule — internal helpers, test access via `export_test.go` aliases) and lowercase the env-parse warning messages, per bot review.\n\n## v0.3.11\n\n- chore: update Go to 1.26.6 and dependencies; fix GO-2026-5841 (klauspost/compress), GO-2026-6179 (golang.org/x/mod), GO-2026-6180 (golang.org/x/mod), GO-2026-5026 (stdlib), GO-2026-5972 (stdlib), GO-2026-6090 (stdlib), GO-2026-6218 (stdlib)\n\n## v0.3.10\n\n- fix: fail-fast on a permanently-failed prompt instead of hanging to the lifecycle deadline. When a dark-factory prompt exhausts its retries the failure handler leaves it in `prompts/in-progress` with `status: failed` and the scanner skips it forever (\"queue blocked until manual retry\"), so its spec can never reach `verifying`. `drained()` could not distinguish that dead prompt from a slow one, so `waitForDrain` spun until the 30-minute `lifecycleTimeout` — and in-cluster the overall-run budget hard-killed the Job with `context canceled` at ~30 min BEFORE the step could escalate, so no `## Failure` was ever written and the task was not cleanly parked. `waitForDrain` now scans `prompts/in-progress` each tick via new `findFailedPrompt` and returns a lifecycle error the moment a prompt reaches terminal `status: failed`, surfacing its `lastFailReason`. The execution step then escalates cleanly (`Status: failed`) within seconds while the context is still alive — PR stays draft, spec stays incomplete, task parks. Approved/executing/unparseable prompts are ignored (only terminal `failed` counts). Unit tests cover the detector's discrimination + the RunLifecycle fast-path timing.\n\n## v0.3.9\n\n- fix: commit the spec-completion move before pushing, so completed specs actually reach the PR branch. `dark-factory spec complete` (which the execution step drives after the daemon stops) rewrites the working tree — moving the spec `specs/in-progress → specs/completed` — but does NOT git-commit it (nothing commits it once the daemon is gone, unlike the daemon's per-prompt `workflow:direct` commits). `PushBranch` is a bare `git push HEAD`, so the completion was silently dropped: the PR kept an approved-not-completed spec, the watcher re-emitted the task on the agent's own push, and runs overlapped indefinitely (self-trigger loop). New `ExecutionRunner.CommitSpecChanges` stages `specs/` and commits after `completeSpecs` and before `PushBranch` (no-op when nothing is staged). Now the completed spec lands on the branch → the watcher stops re-triggering → clean multi-spec drain to `human_review`. Unit tests cover the commit + the no-op path and assert the execution step calls it before pushing.\n\n## v0.3.8\n\n- fix: `RunLifecycle` no longer stops the dark-factory daemon while a generated prompt is still awaiting auto-approval. The drain check (`drained()`) only looked at `prompts/in-progress`, ignoring the generation **inbox** (`prompts/*.md` at the repo root, where a freshly generated prompt waits for the daemon's auto-approve audit). Because the daemon flips the spec to `verifying` while that audit runs, `drained()` returned true mid-audit, `stopDaemon` SIGKILLed the in-flight audit subprocess (`auto-approve: audit FAILED ... signal: killed`), the prompt was never approved/executed, and the implementation file (e.g. the `HELLO_DARK_FACTORY.md` marker) was never created — ai_review then correctly escalated on the absent file. New `hasInboxPrompts` gates the drain on the inbox too, so the lifecycle waits for the prompt to be approved → executed → completed. Surfaced on go-skeleton PR #40 (master `.dark-factory.yaml` sets `validationPrompt: docs/dod.md`, making the audit slow enough to lose the race). Unit test drives a stub daemon that leaves a prompt in the inbox and asserts the lifecycle does not drain.\n\n## v0.3.7\n\n- fix: inject the fleet model into the backend:local daemon. The execution step now forwards the configured model (`claudelib.ClaudeModel`, from the pod's `ANTHROPIC_MODEL`) to `dark-factory daemon` as `--set model=<model>` via `NewExecutionStep`'s new constructor arg — no `os.Getenv` inside the step. The daemon resolves its model from `--set` > `.dark-factory.yaml` `model:` > a built-in Claude default; because it does NOT read `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` for its own selection, a target repo whose `.dark-factory.yaml` omits `model:` silently ran on Sonnet (a ToU violation), which on go-skeleton PR #40 produced a prompt asserting the wrong byte count and never committed the marker. Injecting the model makes the fleet model authoritative regardless of per-repo config. Unit test asserts the flag is forwarded.\n\n## v0.3.6\n\n- fix: ai_review no longer flags dark-factory's own bookkeeping as out-of-scope. The read-only diff-vs-spec reviewer (`pkg/prompts/review.md`) now excludes pipeline-metadata paths — `prompts/**`, `specs/**`, and `.dark-factory.yaml` — from its scope judgment, since the lifecycle necessarily commits them on every run (generated prompts, in-progress→completed moves). Previously a correct implementation escalated to `failed` at ai_review because a spec constraint like \"No other file is modified\" can never hold for this pipeline (observed in cluster E2E: the marker was created correctly but the reviewer flagged the prompt/spec churn). The reviewer now judges only real implementation files against the spec. Verified via the local `cmd/run-task` loop against a clean draft PR: the same scenario now returns `pass` → `human_review`.\n- chore: `go mod tidy` records `github.com/maxbrunsfeld/counterfeiter/v6` as an indirect dependency.\n- chore: bump `golang.org/x/text` v0.38.0 → v0.39.0 (indirect) — clears CVE-2026-56852 (infinite loop on invalid input) flagged by trivy.\n\n## v0.3.5\n\n- chore: bump baked `DARK_FACTORY_VERSION` v0.192.0 → v0.192.4 (CLI + plugin). v0.192.4 fixes the `generate-prompts-for-spec` command's hardcoded `/workspace` paths (docker-mount convention) → cwd-relative, so backend:local generation writes prompts into the worktree the daemon watches instead of an empty `/workspace`. Unblocks the backend:local generation→execution lifecycle.\n\n## v0.3.4\n\n- fix: populate remote-tracking refs (`refs/remotes/origin/*`) in the RepoManager's bare clone. `git clone --bare` maps remote branches to `refs/heads/*`, so `origin/master` never resolved in a worktree — and dark-factory's per-prompt \"sync with default branch\" (`git merge origin/<default>`, run by every workflow) failed with `origin/master - not something we can merge`, stalling execution after generation. New `configureRemoteTracking` sets the standard `+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*` refspec and fetches, on both the clone and update paths.\n\n## v0.3.3\n\n- fix: bake the `coding` plugin into the runtime image (Dockerfile), alongside the existing `dark-factory` plugin. dark-factory's generation step invokes the prompt-creator agent, which uses the `coding` plugin's Go guides to write prompt files; without it the fleet's MiniMax model (`MiniMax-M2.7-highspeed`) produced no prompt file (\"generation produced no prompt files\") and the spec never left `approved`. Local runs generate fine because the operator's `~/.claude` already has `coding`; the cluster image did not. Mirrors github-pr-review-agent's build-time `coding` install.\n\n## v0.3.2\n\n- fix: stream the `dark-factory` daemon's stdout/stderr to the agent's own stdout in `dark_factory_runner.startDaemon` (was `cmd.Stdout/Stderr = nil`, i.e. discarded). The backend:local lifecycle — and the claude subprocesses the daemon spawns — is now visible in `kubectl logs`. Without it a hung daemon (e.g. a claude call blocking on a no-TTY onboarding prompt) was indistinguishable from slow progress until the 30-minute lifecycle deadline. Writes to the process stdout/stderr, not a repo file, so it is not swept into the daemon's commits.\n\n## v0.3.1\n\n- fix: hoist `ARG CLAUDE_YOLO_IMAGE` to global scope (before the first `FROM`) in the Dockerfile. It was declared inside the build stage, so the runtime `FROM ${CLAUDE_YOLO_IMAGE}` could not interpolate it and BuildKit aborted with \"base name should not be blank\" — `make buca` failed before any push. CI runs `make precommit` (go build/test/lint), never `docker build`, so this latent bug surfaced only on the first real image build.\n- fix: build the dark-factory CLI from the pinned clone (`go install .` in the module root) instead of `go install github.com/bborbe/dark-factory@${DARK_FACTORY_VERSION}`. dark-factory's `go.mod` carries `exclude` directives, which `go install pkg@version` refuses; building from the module root honors them (same as upstream `make install`). Reuses the clone the runtime image already makes for the plugin marketplace, so there is now a single pinned clone for both CLI and plugin. Both fixes verified with a local `make build`.\n\n## v0.3.0\n\n- feat: add GitHub App authentication. When `APP_ID`, `INSTALLATION_ID`, and a PEM (`PEM_KEY_FILE` or `PEM_KEY`) are all set, the agent mints a short-lived installation access token at startup (`githubapp.MintIAT`) and forwards it to every git/gh subprocess — `os.Setenv(\"GH_TOKEN\", …)` covers the dark-factory daemon's `git push` (inherits `os.Environ()`), `gh auth setup-git` installs the git credential helper, and the token is threaded into the RepoManager, GitHub REST client, and gh-token preflight. Falls back to the raw `GH_TOKEN` input when App creds are absent so local `cmd/run-task` (with `GH_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)`) keeps working; errors clearly when neither is configured. Fixes cluster clone/push failures where the pod set no `GH_TOKEN`. Copies `pkg/githubauth` (the `gh auth setup-git` configurator) from github-pr-review-agent.\n\n- fix: add `--set hideGit=true` to the execution step's dark-factory flags — the RepoManager runs the lifecycle in a git worktree (`.git` is a file), which trips dark-factory's spec-084 worktree safety gate; without this the daemon refuses to start. Validated in the local E2E: the daemon now starts and runs inside the worktree.\n- fix: install the dark-factory Claude PLUGIN in the runtime image (Dockerfile), not just the CLI binary. The backend:local lifecycle runs `/dark-factory:generate-prompts-for-spec` and `/dark-factory:audit-prompt` *inside* claude; with only the CLI installed, claude reports `Unknown command` → zero prompts generated → the spec silently resets to `approved` and the daemon idles (the E2E root cause, 2026-07-13). Mirrors github-pr-review-agent's build-time `coding` plugin install. **Pinned** to the same `${DARK_FACTORY_VERSION}` tag as the CLI (clone that tag + add as a local marketplace, not marketplace HEAD) so the plugin minor cannot drift from the CLI minor. Confirmed locally: with the plugin installed, generation produces a valid prompt.\n- docs: record two backend:local provisioning requirements in design.md Part 5 — (1) the dark-factory plugin must be installed in the runtime `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`; (2) backend:local ignores dark-factory `config.env`, so the model-router `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` + auth token must be pod env vars (a spec-104 follow-up gap).\n\n## v0.2.0\n\n- feat: implement Increment 1 of the three-phase implementer — wire planning/execution/ai_review as distinct phases (domain.TaskPhase constants), route the `dark-factory-implement` task type; add RepoManager (bare-clone cache + per-task worktree), claude-auth + gh-token preflight steps, and the pure-Go planning step (clone + precondition scan: ref==PR head, PR draft, `.dark-factory.yaml` present, approved-not-completed spec in the PR diff → writes `## Plan`, routes execution; escalates without mutating assignee/status); wire `cmd/run-task` as a local per-phase harness. Execution/ai_review are stubs (Increments 2/3).\n- feat: implement Increment 2 — the execution step drives the dark-factory backend:local lifecycle. Live investigation established the correct one-worktree invocation: `dark-factory daemon --set backend=local --set autoGeneratePrompts=true --auto-approve-prompts --skip-preflight --skip-healthcheck` (backgrounded → poll-until-drained → kill; one-shot `run` does NOT generate — generation is daemon-only), then `dark-factory spec complete <id>` (no auto-complete), then `git push`. Adds `pkg/dark_factory_runner.go` (`ExecutionRunner` seam over daemon/spec-complete/push) and replaces the execution stub: idempotent (`## Result` present → route ai_review), config via `--set`/flags never committed to the branch, fail-closed when a spec never reaches `verifying`/`completed` (prompt failed DoD/audit), writes typed `## Result`, routes ai_review — all without mutating assignee/status.\n- feat: implement Increment 3 — the ai_review step (custom verdict-routing, read-only). A deterministic gate re-checks post-conditions (PR still draft, every matched spec completed, no prompts in-flight) and escalates (`failed`) on violation; then a read-only Claude diff-vs-spec review emits a typed `## Review` verdict. Pass → `Done, human_review` (the human's draft→ready flip is the sign-off); concerns → `failed` (escalate). NEVER runs `gh pr ready`; never clears assignee (the controller does at human_review). Completes the three-phase implementer agent logic (planning → execution → ai_review).\n\n## v0.1.3\n\n- docs: mark the local-executor dependency RESOLVED in design.md and record the backend:local live-smoke findings — happy path (claude in-process, real commit, zero containers) + the HOME-sensitive claude-auth requirement the Job pod must satisfy\n\n## v0.1.2\n\n- chore: bump dark-factory CLI to v0.192.0 (ships spec 104 `backend: local` in-process executor) so the agent runs dark-factory without spawning nested containers (no DinD); select at runtime with `--set backend=local`\n\n## v0.1.1\n\n- refactor: converge build to bborbe/kafka-topic-reader publish-only model — make buca publishes docker.io/bborbe/github-dark-factory-agent:$(VERSION); deploy machinery removed.\n\n## v0.1.0\n\n- feat: adopt cqrs v0.6.0 / agent v0.72.0 explicit `base.TopicPrefix`; add optional `TopicPrefix` config (`env TOPIC_PREFIX`) for Kafka result topic naming — empty means unprefixed topics (Octopus per-stage clusters), non-empty preserves `develop`/`master` names (quant)\n- chore: bump `github.com/bborbe/agent` v0.70.0 → v0.72.0, `github.com/bborbe/cqrs` v0.5.2 → v0.6.0\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":7,"oldLines":7,"newStart":7,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," ## Unreleased"," ","-- feat: route the GitHub REST client and the GH-token preflight through `bborbe/http`'s rate-limit retry round tripper (`github.com/bborbe/http` v1.28.1), so a 403/429 from `api.github.com` is retried after the server-requested wait instead of ending the work item; waits over the five-second `MaxWait` bound are deferred so the caller sees the real rate-limit response rather than a context-deadline error","+- feat: route the GitHub REST client and the GH-token preflight through `bborbe/http`'s rate-limit retry round tripper (`github.com/bborbe/http` v1.28.1), so a 403/429 from `api.github.com` is retried after the server-requested wait instead of ending the work item; the per-wait `MaxWait` bound is one second so the total sleep budget across the default five attempts (5 × 1s) stays inside both client timeouts, and a longer server-requested wait is deferred so the caller sees the real rate-limit response rather than a context-deadline error"," "," ## v0.8.7"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
